# LithoAI – AI-Based Semiconductor Process Monitoring

## Project Overview

LithoAI is a research project exploring the use of artificial intelligence and sensor data for semiconductor manufacturing process monitoring.

The long-term research focuses on three areas:

1. AI-based defect prediction using manufacturing sensor data.
2. Overlay error prediction in semiconductor lithography.
3. Optical Physical Unclonable Functions (PUFs).

## Current Implementation

The first experimental prototype uses the SECOM Semiconductor Manufacturing Dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.

The current implementation focuses on predicting semiconductor manufacturing Pass/Fail outcomes using sensor data.

## Methodology

- Dataset exploration and preprocessing
- Missing-value analysis and mean imputation
- Removal of constant features
- Feature standardization
- Handling class imbalance using SMOTE
- Logistic Regression classification
- Validation-based threshold selection
- Final evaluation on an independent test set

## Dataset

- Original samples: 1,567
- Original sensor features: 590
- Features retained after missing-value filtering: 566
- Final features after constant-feature removal: 444

## Experimental Results

**Model:** Logistic Regression with SMOTE

**Selected threshold:** 0.1

| Metric | Test Result |
|---|---:|
| Accuracy | 79.62% |
| Fail Precision | 14% |
| Fail Recall | 38% |
| Fail F1-score | 20% |
| Average Precision | 0.1115 |

The model detected 8 out of 21 failure cases in the test dataset.

## Limitations

- The SECOM dataset contains Pass/Fail labels rather than direct lithography defect labels.
- It does not provide direct overlay error measurements.
- The dataset is highly imbalanced.
- The current model produces a high number of false-positive predictions.
- Overlay prediction and optical PUF research remain separate future research directions.

